00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.000
And we'll go to Windows application, which will basically just give us our own sort of,

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.000
I don't know why that thing keeps going.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.000
This will basically give us our own sort of dumping ground where we can put stuff

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.000
in and build it.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.000
Now the way that Microsoft Visual Studio works is it has this thing called a solution

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
explorer.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.000
And one thing that will often happen to you when you're developing is it will

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
go away.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.000
something that's like this or perhaps you'll get something that's even completely blank,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:14.000
so that has nothing in it like this. And you can get back to this solution explorer thing by going

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.000
to view solution explorer. Now what is the solution you may ask? What was the problem even? You may

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:26.000
also ask and that's a very valid question. But the answer is there wasn't necessarily a problem

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.000
unless you consider the problem being that Microsoft makes it very hard to build things.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.000
Solution was just something that they started calling a project at some point.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:39.000
And so really a solution is just their word for a thing that you are going to build.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.000
It's like a basically a list of things that need to get built and how they're going to build them.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:49.000
And so instead of having an intuitive name like files explorer or project explorer,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.000
it's called solution explorer and you have to go to it.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:58.000
When you go to the solution explorer, you will basically get something here that works a lot like,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.000
you know, the file explorer in Windows.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.000
It's hierarchical and you can collapse it.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.000
And they made some folders in here, but we don't really have to care about these folders very much.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.000
And we can add our own folders or rename folders.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:17.000
So if we wanted to rename one of these, you know, we could go in here and call this whatever we wanted and that's fine.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.000
But what we need to do in order to start building something is actually have some files in here to build

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.000
so that it will know what we are trying to build.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.000
So in order to do that, I'm just going to right-click on this

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.000
and go into Add, and I'm going to pick New Item.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.000
So that will let us make a new file, basically,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.000
that we can actually build.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.000
So that will pop up a massive window of stuff,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.000
because there's all sorts of things

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:41.000
that you might want to do.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.000
You might want to make an SQL script file or a class

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.000
diagram.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
Those might be good things to make,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.000
but we're not going to make those right now.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.000
Instead, we're going to make just a straight up CPP file.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.000
OK.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.000
So basically, I always forget to enter the name, don't I?

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.000
So basically we just have to give our C file a name and add it.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
Now we will have a blank file that we can start typing into.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.000
And that is what we will use to start programming in C.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.000
Now, programming in C has not advanced much over the years,

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.000
unfortunately.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Obviously C and C++, they're both pretty old.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.000
But I think C++ was sometime early 80s.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.000
C was a good decade before that, I want to say.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:27.000
And yet, just the same, even though they're so old,

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.000
the way in which you actually enter C code into a computer

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:35.000
and the way it is actually built is still almost the same

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.000
as it was back then.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.000
You basically just have text files.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.000
They're basic plain old text files,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.000
less complicated than something

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.000
that you would type into Microsoft Word.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.000
Like they literally don't even have annotation or anything.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.000
They're just raw text.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:54.000
And that raw text is then interpreted by the tools

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.000
to do the things that we need to do.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.000
And that's done with the compiler and stuff

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.000
that we'll be talking about in a second.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.000
But basically just understand that nothing we're going to do

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.000
is going to be anything other than entering text

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.000
into a computer.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:07.000
OK.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.000
So the first thing to do is to say, well, OK,

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:13.000
we haven't actually done anything here.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:18.000
This is some text in a file.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.000
And what you will notice is that this basically,

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
every time I type in here, there's a little yellow bar

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.000
that sort of appears next to it.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.000
And that just tells you that these are new lines that you

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.000
have typed since you have saved.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.000
And you can see that I've got a little star there that now

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.000
adds to it, just like in most applications that says,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.000
this file has been modified since it has been saved.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:43.000
Now, if I want to save it, I can just go to file save test.cpp.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.000
Or if I want it some shorthand, I can hit Control S.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:47.000
And there it saves it.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:51.000
And you'll notice that the yellow bars now change to a green bar.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.000
And what does a green bar mean?

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.000
Well, a green bar is basically something

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.000
that lets you know that although the file is saved,

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.000
there was still a change recently there.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.000
And I believe, although I am not a frequent user

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.000
of Visual Studio, that it will basically

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:07.000
remove the green bar once we actually compile this

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.000
into a working program so that you know what changes you've

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.000
made since the last time you've updated your program.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.000
OK.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:20.000
So let's say I want to actually run the process of making something out of this text file.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Well, the thing that you do there is you say build solution.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:27.000
And build solution will go through everything that we have in here.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:32.000
And it will actually do the work necessary to make this in to a program.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.000
And we'll talk about all those steps in a little while.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.000
So when I hit build solution, you will notice that if I did not have an output window open,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.000
it magically made one for me.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.000
It just pops up. There's really nothing you can do about it.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:51.000
And what it does is it prints out what happened when it tried to build the program.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:55.000
So it starts out by giving me a line which says what it was trying to build.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.000
So it says build started project test.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:04.000
Now that's what we wanted. You can see in here we've essentially when it created our automatic project for us,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:09.000
it created a solution called test and it put inside it one default project which is called test.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:13.000
And the reason I call it a default project, you can see it's bold, is because you can

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:18.000
actually go in here and there's a thing called Set as Startup Project and some other things

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.000
like this.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:24.000
There's basically a notion of what the project is you're going to build when you build

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.000
the solution.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:28.000
Because we could actually stack other projects in here that build other programs, which

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.000
we don't all want to build and other sorts of nonsense like this.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.000
Most of that is not really necessary when you're doing development unless you start

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.000
developing on a very large and complex project.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.000
But if you're just building a game by yourself,

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.000
it's unlike you'll need to ever do anything like that.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.000
And I often don't build anything from here.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.000
So one of the things we're going to cover later

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.000
is how to build things without using this.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.000
But that said, test is the project

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.000
that we will build when we do build solution.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.000
And so what this was telling us here

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.000
is it was going to build that project.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.000
And there's a configuration line which says debug win32.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.000
And what the configuration means

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.000
is basically what sort of build it was trying to do.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Because as we'll see later on, there's actually

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.000
multiple ways you can build the same set of C code.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.000
Some of them are designed to target different platforms.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.000
For example, you may want to target a 32-bit copy of Windows

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.000
or a 64-bit copy of Windows.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.000
And that would be part of the configuration.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.000
You might also be trying to do things

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.000
that are based on a debug developer build, so something

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:35.000
that has a lot of test code compiled into it.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.000
Special menus for you, the editor for the game

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:40.000
or something might be built in this mode.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.000
But then there might be another build called

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Release or Retail, depending on how you do your naming scheme,

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.000
which will build these things out.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.000
And so basically, configuration is something

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.000
that affects the way in which these text files are actually

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.000
interpreted by the various things that we do in order

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:02.000
to get to an application that is somehow different

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.000
from the other configurations that we might build.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.000
So now the next line you can see here is compiling.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.000
And what that is, is compilation is the general term.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.000
It's not a particularly specific term,

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.000
although we actually use it to mean something specific oftentimes.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:20.000
It's actually a very general term that technically means moving

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:23.000
something from one format into another, basically.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:27.000
It's sort of like a translation in some sense.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:31.000
A compiler is something that parses a text file typically.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.000
It turns it into something semantic.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.000
It understands the meaning of that text file,

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.000
what we were trying to do with that text file.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:42.000
And then it turns it into some other thing that we wanted.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.000
Now, in this case, what it's going to do

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.000
is we're actually trying to get it to produce,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:50.000
at the end of the day, an actual program that we can run.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.000
It's executable, something that you would click on in Windows

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:57.000
to run, like with the little icon, dot exe, that thing.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.000
We want to build that thing.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:01.000
And in order to build that thing,

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.000
Basically, the text code that we write

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.000
has to be translated into something

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.000
that the computer can run.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.000
And that is what the compilation step does.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.000
And there's another step after compilation

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:12.000
that we'll talk about.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.000
But the compilation step is basically

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.000
in charge of doing the majority of the work

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:20.000
to translate our text file from something humans read

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.000
into something that a CPU can read,

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:27.000
and specifically oftentimes a specific processor,

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:28.000
meaning the compilation will be

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:33.000
compiling from a text file into say x86 code or x64 code,

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:37.000
something for the actual CPU that you are trying to target,

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.000
not abstractly.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:44.000
So given that, after we see the compilation line,

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
we know the compiler has started, and we now

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.000
go to a list of what files it's actually compiling.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:53.000
Now we only have one file, which is test.cpp.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:56.000
And so not surprisingly, it tries to compile that file.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Now you will note in front of all of these,

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:04.000
there is a little number with a sort of greater than symbol.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:08.000
And what that is is totally irrelevant for the most part

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.000
to what you're going to be doing for development.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.000
It's not really something you need to pay attention to,

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:14.000
but just in case you were wondering,

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.000
if you are on a machine that happens

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.000
to have a lot of processors.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:22.000
So if I go in here, if I basically just

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000
used the hotkey, one of my favorite hotkeys,

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.000
Control Shift Escape to bring up the Task Manager,

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:32.000
You will notice that this machine has a lot of processors.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:35.000
In this thing called CPU usage history,

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:39.000
there is one of these for every logical processor

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:41.000
in the machine.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:44.000
Now, a logical processor is, or I shouldn't say logical

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:47.000
processor, perhaps that's the wrong word.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:48.000
I'm going to stick with logical processor.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.000
Now, logical processor is not an individual processor.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:54.000
It's basically a logical unit that can run code.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.000
And there's two different kinds of these.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.000
And we might as well talk about them now,

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:01.000
but I'm going to talk about them certainly more later on

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.000
in the actual series, not in this introductory series.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.000
But basically, the way a processor is divided internally,

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.000
or the way your computer is divided internally,

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.000
obviously, there are actual physical processors in there.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.000
So most people have one physical processor

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.000
in their machine, obviously.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.000
But other machines have two or three or four,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.000
maybe even dozens of processors in them.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Certainly not consumer boxes, but certain server types

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:27.000
might have 32 processors in a machine, for example.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.000
Those physical processors obviously are chips they were made by Intel.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.000
They are stuck in the machine.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:37.000
They have a nice big old heat sink on them to compete to prevent them from overheating.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:43.000
And when you power on your machine, they are what executes everything except the graphics code or things like the networking,

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:46.000
which is sometimes on separate cards or separate chips.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.000
The CPU is what does all the work.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:52.000
But in addition to being a physical unit,

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:56.000
the CPU itself is actually divided internally into processing units,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:00.000
which actually can run code sort of separately.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.000
Those are typically called cores.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:07.000
So inside a single CPU, you might have four cores,

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.000
or eight cores, or something like this.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.000
And you will see these oftentimes advertised in Intel advertising,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.000
and of course more cores is better, but what is a core?

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.000
Well, a core is basically something that is capable

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:22.000
of doing the work of a standalone CPU for the most part.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.000
It is something that would have been a CPU in the old days

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:29.000
when CPUs could only execute one thing at a time.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:31.000
Now you have processors capable of executing multiple things

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:32.000
at a time.

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:36.000
And each one of those units is called a core inside.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:39.000
And the cores typically have a bunch of things in them.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.000
They have units capable of doing mathematics.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:44.000
They have units capable of doing memory addressing

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:45.000
all these sorts of things.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:48.000
And then they will talk to the memory in your system.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.000
They'll talk to the bus.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.000
They'll talk to all sorts of things in the system

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:53.000
in order to do their job.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:20:00.000
by and large a core is the thing that actually does the computation, but even that is still not really

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:08.000
what you might call a logical processing unit nowadays. And the reason for that is they've subdivided that even further

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:12.000
down to things called hyper threads. And of course,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:19.000
hyper threads are basically a thing where a processor's core

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.000
is actually able to maintain the state

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:28.000
of multiple cores as if it were multiple cores,

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:30.000
even though it is only capable

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.000
of executing any one given core at a time.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:36.000
So for example, you think of a CPU core

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.000
as being able to remember, say, what it was running,

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.000
where it is in the program,

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.000
what the state of the things it was it was operating on,

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.000
these sorts of things,

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:46.000
and we'll get into what all of those are in detail later.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
But a core is basically doing this work

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.000
of running an application.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:54.000
that core can actually store multiple of those states,

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.000
almost like a multitasking operating system you can think of as

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:01.000
there's the task bar and it's got different tasks that it has.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:06.000
Well, a core can actually do that tasking in a sense itself.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:09.000
Even though it's only running one thing at a given time,

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:16.000
it could keep say four additional states that it can really quickly switch to.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:20.000
And so what happens is those actually show up, often times,

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:23.000
in the operating system as a logical processing unit,

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:27.000
because to the operating system, they sort of look like a processor.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.000
They are effectively running something,

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.000
even though only one of them on a particular core

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:34.000
is actually inflated at a given time.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.000
Now, there's tons more to talk about there,

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:38.000
but I am prone to tangent, so I thought

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:40.000
I'd mention some of that when I booted up here.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.000
So as you can see, there are a lot of logical processing

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.000
units on this.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:48.000
this many processors in the actual machine. In fact I don't actually know

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:56.000
if I can go on here. I think, let me see, if this actually will tell us what kind

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:03.000
of processor it is. Yes, it is a Xeon. It is a two processor box, although it

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.000
doesn't actually tell me how many threads per, I mean how many cores per

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:10.000
processor it actually has. But we could go look up what a W5580 is and we

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:15.000
You could see how many it has, but I think it's probably only got maybe four per, I don't

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:17.000
know, I'm not really sure.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:22.000
But anyway, each one of these would correspond basically to one individual, one of those

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:27.000
hyper threads, and it would show me basically how much usage that particular thread is

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.000
getting right now.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Okay, so hopefully that gives you a little bit of perspective on this.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:37.000
I wonder too, you know what, nowadays they've kind of added to this thing.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:40.000
I wonder too if there's any way I can get more information

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:43.000
about things like hyper threads on these.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.000
Like if they even show hyper threads,

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:47.000
I feel like in the old days you could toggle this

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.000
and say whether you were showing processors

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:50.000
or logical processors.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:51.000
I don't know.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.000
I do not know.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:00.000
No, it doesn't look like there's much here for that.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.000
Yeah.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:02.000
All right.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:04.000
Well, anyway, that is not really relevant to what we're doing.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:05.000
So I'm going to skip it.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:06.000
But maybe later on we will go try

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.000
to find out how to get a little more information

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:10.000
and take a look at those things.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.000
Anyway, so there are a lot of processors in this system.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:17.000
And when you build something, obviously,

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:18.000
compilers can get a little slow.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.000
And if you have a lot of things to compile,

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:21.000
they could take some time.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.000
And so one of the things that compilers do

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:25.000
is they are multi-threaded sometimes,

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.000
depending on which one you're talking about.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:31.000
And they will try to compile using more than one logical

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:32.000
processing unit.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:36.000
So they'll try to use more of the CPU resources

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.000
to get the compile done faster.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:41.000
But this little thing here represents, if I remember correctly,

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:43.000
it's been a long time since I've ever really used one of these.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:51.000
It just lets you know a sequence number of basically which sort of spawn of the compiler

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.000
was reporting this message.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:58.000
Because you may have multiple compilations going, multiple different CPUs doing compilations.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:03.000
And I believe that basically just tells you which one you're looking at at that time.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:06.000
So if we were to do a more complicated compilation with multiple files,

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:10.000
3s 4s going on there as it spooled off jobs in a more complex fashion. But for

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:13.000
the moment we're only seeing ones because it only had one file to compile and

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:19.000
did not bother to launch extra threads to do that. Anyway, so this is the file

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:23.000
that was compiling and we will see here the first time we got an error. And

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:27.000
what an error is is an error means basically it was trying to read the

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:31.000
text file that we gave it and it did not like what it found. Now that is

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:33.000
unsurprising because I basically just typed a bunch of gibberish in here.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:37.000
but what we can do is when we double click on this it will tell us where the

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:42.000
problem is. It will then stop basically what it's doing. So when you have errors

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:46.000
it will list the errors that it found, it will list them up usually to a

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.000
certain number of errors and usually that's parameterizable, but after it

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:52.000
gets to a certain number of errors it will stop the compilation and it will

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:56.000
basically just report that it failed the compilation and you can see it

00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:01.000
says one errors here and it says zero succeeded one failed and you know

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:03.000
It lets us know if they're ones that it skipped because it

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.000
didn't think it needed to do any work, that sort of thing.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:07.000
And it'll also save a build log for us,

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:10.000
which are pretty useless most of the time.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.000
But if you want to look at the build log,

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.000
basically it saves an HTML file that

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:17.000
has sort of a log of what happened in the build for,

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:18.000
I guess, for posterity.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.000
Or if you want to do some kind of thing where you save

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.000
build logs or do other kinds of stuff with that.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.000
Maybe you are running these in some way

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:26.000
that you're viewing remotely.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:27.000
There's reasons for build logs,

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.000
but we're certainly not going to have any use for it.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.000
OK.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.000
So that rather exciting process there where we just hit build

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:37.000
and we get an error is what we're going to be doing

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:38.000
in order to produce an executable.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.000
And in order to get to the next step of actually producing

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:45.000
an executable, what we are going to have to do

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:49.000
is we are going to have to give it some code that it actually

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.000
understands.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:55.000
And basically, I'm going to type in some wrote code now.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:57.000
And then I'm going to explain to you sort of what it does.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.000
It's sort of the simplest thing

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:02.000
that we can possibly type in to get to the next stage.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:05.000
And here it is.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:10.000
So if I now try to build this, you will notice,

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.000
and don't worry, we'll get to this in a second,

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.000
you will notice that what it does is it gets past

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:17.000
that step we were before.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.000
We got to test.cpp.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:22.000
And it actually was able to sort of move past that

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:24.000
and move on to doing other things.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:28.000
Now, it tried to launch the Microsoft resource compiler.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:31.000
And that was because we have a resource files thing in here

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.000
and it created this project for us

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:34.000
and it seems we're gonna have them.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:36.000
A resource compiler is nothing fancy.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:38.000
It sounds a lot better than it is.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:39.000
You're like, oh, resource compiler.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:40.000
I could compile my game's resource with that.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:44.000
Not very likely, and you probably don't want to do that

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:47.000
for myriad reasons, but what the resource compiler

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:50.000
actually does is the resource compiler does things

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:54.000
like packing up an icon for your application

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:59.000
and sticking it in somewhere where Windows can find it

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:00.000
in the executable so that it can display it

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.000
next year executable and things like that.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.000
So it is something that you do need to know how to do.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:06.000
Eventually, but right now we don't care at all.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.000
Obviously it didn't need to actually do anything,

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:09.000
so it finished that up.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.000
But then it got to the step that I actually wanted

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:13.000
to talk about, which is linking.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:19.000
Now what linking is, is linking is sort of a back half

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.000
to this compilation process.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:26.000
Now what we could do, a compiler certainly could take a text file

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:28.000
and it could directly make an executable out of it

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.000
if that text file contained everything that it needed

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.000
to define the executable.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:37.000
But what usually happens more often, and partly for legacy reasons

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:41.000
at this point, but what can you do?

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:44.000
What happens more often is you have a lot of text files.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.000
So the code for a game could be hundreds of thousands

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.000
of lines of code.

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:49.000
It could be split up into lots of these CPP files

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.000
that we're going to learn how to write here.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:57.000
And in order to quote unquote save compilation time

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:00.000
and various other reasons, what it often

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.000
wants to do is be able to compile each file

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:06.000
into an intermediate file doing one part of the compilation

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.000
process, one part of the producing executable.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:10.000
And that intermediate file will basically

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:12.000
be the process that takes the text

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:16.000
and turns it into mostly code that the CPU connects to you.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:20.000
but there are a bunch of things that aren't quite done yet,

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:23.000
then those intermediate files don't always have to be updated.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:26.000
They only have to be updated when the particular source file

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:27.000
that they came from changed.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:31.000
So for example, this test.cpp, if I don't change test.cpp,

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.000
it won't have to recompile test.cpp.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:36.000
If I had tons of other files, foo.cpp, bar.cpp,

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:39.000
all these other CPP files, and I changed just one of them,

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.000
it would only have to do the recompilation on just one

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:42.000
of them.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:44.000
But since all of them have to be pulled in and made

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:47.000
into an executable at the end, they basically

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:49.000
have a second stage to the compilation process,

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:50.000
which is called linking.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:52.000
And that's what this line does here.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.000
And what linking is about is linking

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:57.000
is about gathering all of that processing together,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:01.000
all of those intermediate files, and basically resolving

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:05.000
the places where they point to each other.

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:09.000
So I may do a bunch of things in test.cpp

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:13.000
that are going to call out and or reference to,

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:19.000
or use data from other CPP files that I compiled.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:22.000
And since the compiler is only looking

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:25.000
at each individual CPP file as it goes and compiles them,

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.000
it can't resolve those references.

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:30.000
It can't tell where the things are

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.000
that things are pointed to because it's only

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:33.000
seeing one individual file.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:36.000
And so what the linker does in this link stage,

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:38.000
there's a thing called a linker that gets executed

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:40.000
separate from the compiler.

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:42.000
And what it is, is it is something

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:45.000
that gathers all those together, resolves the references,

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:50.000
packages this into one EXE, which you can then ship.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:53.000
Now, when I say which you can then ship,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.000
that was the good old days.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.000
There's a lot of reasons why you can't actually just ship it,

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.000
thanks to Microsoft and their Tyler's pursuit

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.000
of making building applications complicated.

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:05.000
But I'm gonna hold off on that from now

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:08.000
so that we can focus on just the basics of the process

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:10.000
since it's a lot to absorb

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:11.000
and I don't wanna overcomplicate it

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:13.000
more than it already is.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:14.000
Okay.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:16.000
So in the linking stage,

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.000
you will see that we have gotten to our next error.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:21.000
And that error is this line right here.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:23.000
You'll notice that it doesn't give me a line number

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:25.000
and that is because Microsoft's linker

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.000
is not particularly good.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:28.000
I don't know if they fixed this

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:29.000
in one of the more modern versions,

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:32.000
but even as of 2008,

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.000
their linker never bothered to tell you

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:35.000
where your errors are.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:38.000
So all you get is which function

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:41.000
it was referenced in, if that makes sense.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:45.000
So basically, you will know very little.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.000
You'll know what file maybe made the error possibly,

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.000
and you'll know what function was causing the problem,

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:52.000
or things like that.

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.000
And we'll kind of get to what all those mean

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.000
and how to suss them out later.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.000
But basically, you get very cryptic error message

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:58.000
for a linker.

00:30:57.000 --> 00:31:00.000
Fortunately, most of the time, linker errors

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.000
are simpler errors than compiler errors,

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:06.000
so it's not too hard to figure out what they mean.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.000
So looking at this error, what it says

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:13.000
is unresolved external symbol underscore win main at 16,

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:16.000
reference and function t main CRT startup.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:19.000
It then goes on to tell us that that was a fatal error.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:22.000
So there was one unresolved external.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:24.000
And now what that means, just what I was just saying,

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:27.000
an unresolved external, what external means in this case

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:30.000
is external to the compilation unit

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.000
in which it was being used.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:35.000
So when I compile test.cpp or something,

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:38.000
it's got a corresponding file that the compiler produced

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:40.000
that the linker is then pulling in,

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:43.000
things that are external to that file are called externals.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.000
And when you see one unresolved external,

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:48.000
it means that it came across one of these

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:49.000
that it couldn't resolve.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:52.000
It couldn't find any actual definition of that

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:53.000
anywhere else in the other file.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:54.000
So what can it do?

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.000
It can't finish linking

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:57.000
because it can't figure out

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:00.000
what it was supposed to do there.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:01.000
So again, we get an error.

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:02.000
This time it says two errors,

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.000
but really it was only one error.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:06.000
But that's just the way I guess it decided

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.000
to print it out.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:11.000
what the second error is, but hey, what are you going to do?

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:15.000
Anyway, now that we see what our link error is,

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.000
we can start to debug it.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:20.000
So what actually happens if you think about it

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.000
when you run an executable, there

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:25.000
is something kind of complicated that has to occur,

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:29.000
which is that Windows is actually the program that's

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:30.000
running on your machine.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.000
So the processor in your machine is executing Windows.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.000
And now we have built something

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:38.000
that we would like Windows to start executing.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:44.000
But Windows and our program were not linked together, right?

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:46.000
This linking step that I just explained where, hey,

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:48.000
we can write some stuff and it pulls it all together

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:51.000
and it references each other and everyone's happy.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:54.000
It makes sense if we typed in all the code.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:57.000
But if we only typed in some code that

00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:00.000
has to be started by Windows or started

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.000
by some operating system, well, how does it know where

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:03.000
to start?

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:05.000
How does it actually get started?

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:09.000
And the answer to that is basically that the linking process,

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:12.000
even though it seems like we're going to finish it here in a minute,

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:16.000
is never actually really finished in an executable,

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:19.000
unless what you're building is the whole operating system.

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:23.000
If you are building a kernel that runs the entire machine,

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:25.000
then in some sense you've built the entire program

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:27.000
and linking can resolve all externals.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:30.000
And even that maybe isn't totally true,

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:33.000
because even in that case, there is a BIOS in your computer,

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:37.000
And that BIAS has to know how to load something

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.000
and start executing it, which is the kernel that's on the disk.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:42.000
So it's complicated if you actually

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.000
want to go all the way down to the bottom

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:46.000
and open up the Russian nesting dolls all the way down

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:48.000
to what's actually happening in your machine

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:50.000
when you actually turn it on.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:54.000
But assuming that you're already running a program like Windows,

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:58.000
that's an operating system set up to run executable content,

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:01.000
the question is, how does Windows actually know where to start?

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:04.000
How does it know what to even do with this file?

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:07.000
And the answer is that executable file formats.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:10.000
Executables are files on your disk, just like a bitmap

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.000
or anything else.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:14.000
The executable file format is basically set up

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:16.000
in a way that Windows understands

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:18.000
so that it can open it and read it

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:21.000
and read out of it information

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:25.000
that tells it where to actually start executing.

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:28.000
So when Windows wants to run some of your code,

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:31.000
it basically can load that file into memory,

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:33.000
Do some work on it that it needs to do,

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.000
which we'll talk about again in a little bit.

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:37.000
And then it will use information that

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:41.000
was put in the executable by this linker, in fact,

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:45.000
which tells it where in the file we wanted it to actually

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:46.000
start executing.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.000
So in memory, where should it actually

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:50.000
jump to to actually start executing it?

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.000
Tell the CPU, here's where the code is.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:54.000
Go run it.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:58.000
And the answer to that is, oh, sorry.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:34:59.000
I shouldn't say the answer to that.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:06.000
The way in which we tell the linker where we want it to tell windows where to start is

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:13.000
that we use a predefined name and we name something that name so that it can find it

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.000
in our code.

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:20.000
Now that's not entirely true because we actually could go into the linker itself and there

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:24.000
are settings which we could modify that would tell it to use some other name, for

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:26.000
example, Foo here.

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:29.000
But most of the time, nobody actually does code that way.

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:32.000
Most people, people just use the default name.

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:40.000
And that default name is actually, well, I'm going to say it's WinMain.

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:43.000
As we'll see in a second, it's not actually WinMain, but I don't want to spoil the surprise.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:46.000
So I'm going to say that it's WinMain for now.

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:50.000
And what you can actually do if you, you know, you're not going to probably remember

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:53.000
a lot of this stuff that I'm saying, and I totally respect that.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:56.000
I don't remember all this stuff all the time either.

00:35:56.000 --> 00:36:01.000
I always am looking things up on MSDN, which I will show you in a second.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:05.000
If you type WinMain into your Google search box,

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:09.000
you will get the first hit as this site called msdn.microsoft.com,

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:13.000
which we are going to be using a lot as we learn to program C in Windows

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:17.000
because it is basically a repository for all of the stuff that

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:20.000
is Windows-specific about programming.

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:22.000
And since we're going to be programming in Windows for a while,

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:25.000
that matters to us because we are constantly going to have to look things

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:30.000
up at least in the first couple of weeks, look up things that tell us how to interface

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:36.000
with the operating system. Now in here you can see the definition of WinMain, the documentation

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:40.000
for it, it tells us a bunch of stuff. If you are a beginning C programmer you will understand

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:45.000
none of this, and that is okay. One of the things I am definitely going to do in

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:48.000
this series is go over things many times, we're going to go over things in depth.

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:51.000
So you don't have to worry about the fact that a lot of the stuff that we're

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:57.000
going to do may not be completely understandable to you at the moment. I will always try to

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:01.000
explain as much as you need to know then and then we will go into more detail later as

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:05.000
necessary because I don't want to get too bogged down. I do want to get bogged down fairly

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:09.000
often but I don't want to get too bogged down. And you can also email me anytime

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:13.000
you have a question and we will try to work those questions into future editions

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:20.000
of the series. So this right here is what is called a function prototype and we're

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:21.000
going to be dealing with these a lot.

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:25.000
But basically what it does is it tells you

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.000
the way in which a particular piece of code

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:30.000
needs to start executing.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.000
And we'll get into a lot of stuff with these later.

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:34.000
So I won't belabor the point.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.000
But basically this is the predefined entry point

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:41.000
that we will be getting called at when we start executing.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:44.000
And so if we want to define it in our C program,

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:47.000
we have to basically do something like this.

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:52.000
We have to go in and define this just the way that they did in here.

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:57.000
And when we define this, the linker will now see this symbol, this wind main symbol.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:05.000
And it will know that that is the place that we wanted our execution for Windows to start.

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:09.000
Now, if I go to build again, and you'll see there's a shortcut to it here, which is F7.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:12.000
So it's good to learn this because you build programs a lot.

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:17.000
If I build it with F7, you'll notice one other thing that happened here.

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:19.000
before we get into errors and such.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:20.000
One other thing that happened here is

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:22.000
it saved the file automatically.

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:24.000
I don't know if you noticed the green line,

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:26.000
the yellow line turning green.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:28.000
One of the things that happens by default

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:33.000
in the setup for Microsoft Visual Studio

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:34.000
is that when you build something,

